      "content": "but isn't that kind of a circular way to look at it? you're basically saying the \"official\" version is more logical just because it follows the existing rules, but if those rules were what allowed the fraud to happen in the first place, then the logic falls apart. if someone presented actual evidence of a discrepancy—even if it's just statistical anomalies—wouldn't that make the \"fraud\" argument more logically sound than just assuming everything is fine because the paperwork says so? you seem to be dismissing the entire opposing side's logic as just not having proof, while accepting the official narrative as inherently more rational.",
